# Test Flows

In this tutorial you write ExUnit tests for a Flow: its output and effects, its failures, a stubbed Action, retry behavior, its telemetry, and a managed run. Every test runs offline.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "agentjido/jido", branch: "release/v3"}
])
```

Start ExUnit without its automatic run, so each cell can run its own tests.

```elixir
ExUnit.start(autorun: false)
```

## Define the Flow under test

The Flow approves an expense by scoring it, then records the decision. The score comes from an Action module that a test can replace.

```elixir
defmodule TestFlowsTour.ScoreExpense do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "test_flows_tour_score_expense",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{cents: Jido.Joi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{cents: cents}, _context) when cents < 0 do
    {:error, Jido.Error.execution_error("negative amount", %{reason: :negative_amount, retry: false})}
  end

  def run(%{cents: cents}, _context), do: {:ok, %{score: if(cents > 100_000, do: 10, else: 90)}}
end

defmodule TestFlowsTour.RecordDecision do
  use Jido.Action, name: "test_flows_tour_record_decision"

  @impl true
  def run(%{expense_id: expense_id, score: score}, _context) do
    decision = if score >= 50, do: "approved", else: "review"
    {:ok, %{expense_id: expense_id, decision: decision}, [{:notify_finance, expense_id, decision}]}
  end
end

defmodule TestFlowsTour.ApproveExpense do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "test_flows_tour_approve_expense",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{expense_id: Jido.Joi.string(), cents: Jido.Joi.integer()})

  flow do
    step "score", action: TestFlowsTour.ScoreExpense, params: %{cents: input(:cents)}

    step "record",
      action: TestFlowsTour.RecordDecision,
      params: %{expense_id: input(:expense_id), score: result("score", :score)}

    output result("record")
  end
end

{:ok, _output, [{:notify_finance, "e-0", "approved"}]} =
  Jido.Exec.run(TestFlowsTour.ApproveExpense, %{expense_id: "e-0", cents: 500})
```

## Run the tests

This helper runs the test modules defined so far, then forgets them. In a Mix project, you would put each module in a file under `test/` and run `mix test`.

```elixir
run_tests = fn ->
  result = ExUnit.run()
  %{total: result.total, failures: result.failures}
end
```

## Assert outputs and effects

Call `Jido.Exec.run/4` and match the whole return value. Comparing the full tuple catches an effect you did not expect, because effects change the shape of the result.

```elixir
defmodule TestFlowsTour.OutputTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  test "a small expense is approved and notifies finance" do
    assert Jido.Exec.run(TestFlowsTour.ApproveExpense, %{expense_id: "e-1", cents: 2_000}) ==
             {:ok, %{expense_id: "e-1", decision: "approved"}, [{:notify_finance, "e-1", "approved"}]}
  end

  test "a large expense goes to review" do
    assert {:ok, %{decision: "review"}, _effects} =
             Jido.Exec.run(TestFlowsTour.ApproveExpense, %{expense_id: "e-2", cents: 500_000})
  end
end

%{total: 2, failures: 0} = run_tests.()
```

## Assert failures

Match on the struct and on `details`, not the message. Name the component so the test fails if the failure moves. An Action test proves the Action fails, and this proves the failure stops the Flow and drops its effects.

```elixir
defmodule TestFlowsTour.FailureTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true
  @moduletag capture_log: true

  test "a negative amount fails at the score step" do
    assert {:error, %Jido.Error.ExecutionError{details: details}} =
             Jido.Exec.run(TestFlowsTour.ApproveExpense, %{expense_id: "e-3", cents: -5})

    assert details.reason == :negative_amount
    assert details.node == "score"
  end

  test "invalid input never reaches an Action" do
    assert {:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{details: details}} =
             Jido.Exec.run(TestFlowsTour.ApproveExpense, %{expense_id: "e-4", cents: "lots"})

    assert details.phase == :flow_input
    assert [%{path: [:cents]}] = details.errors
  end
end

%{total: 2, failures: 0} = run_tests.()
```

`@moduletag capture_log: true` keeps log lines from your Actions out of the test output, and prints them when a test fails.

## Replace an Action with a stub

A Flow names its Action modules, so a module Flow cannot swap one at runtime. Pass a dependency through context when the Action can use one. To replace the Action itself, build the same graph as data with `Jido.Flow.new!/1`. The stub is an ordinary Action.

```elixir
defmodule TestFlowsTour.LowScore do
  use Jido.Action, name: "test_flows_tour_low_score"

  @impl true
  def run(_params, _context), do: {:ok, %{score: 1}}
end

defmodule TestFlowsTour.StubTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true
  alias Jido.Flow.Ref

  defp flow_with(scorer) do
    Jido.Flow.new!(%{
      name: "test_flows_tour_stubbed",
      components: [
        %{kind: :step, name: "score", action: scorer, params: %{cents: Ref.input(:cents)}},
        %{
          kind: :step,
          name: "record",
          action: TestFlowsTour.RecordDecision,
          params: %{expense_id: Ref.input(:expense_id), score: Ref.result("score", :score)}
        }
      ],
      output: Ref.result("record")
    })
  end

  test "a low score sends an expense to review" do
    assert {:ok, %{decision: "review"}, _effects} =
             Jido.Exec.run(flow_with(TestFlowsTour.LowScore), %{expense_id: "e-5", cents: 1})
  end
end

%{total: 1, failures: 0} = run_tests.()
```

## Test retries

Count attempts with a process you start in the test, and pass it through context. The default delay is 0, so the test stays fast. These tests also show that `retry: false` stops further attempts.

```elixir
defmodule TestFlowsTour.Gateway do
  use Jido.Action, name: "test_flows_tour_gateway"

  @impl true
  def run(%{fail_times: fail_times, retry: retry}, %{calls: calls}) do
    case Agent.get_and_update(calls, &{&1 + 1, &1 + 1}) do
      call when call <= fail_times ->
        {:error, Jido.Error.execution_error("gateway busy", %{reason: :busy, retry: retry})}

      call ->
        {:ok, %{call: call}}
    end
  end
end

defmodule TestFlowsTour.RetryTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true
  @moduletag capture_log: true

  setup do
    {:ok, calls: start_supervised!({Agent, fn -> 0 end})}
  end

  test "a retryable failure runs again", %{calls: calls} do
    params = %{fail_times: 2, retry: true}

    assert {:ok, %{call: 3}} =
             Jido.Exec.run(TestFlowsTour.Gateway, params, %{calls: calls}, max_attempts: 3)

    assert Agent.get(calls, & &1) == 3
  end

  test "retry: false stops after one attempt", %{calls: calls} do
    params = %{fail_times: 2, retry: false}

    assert {:error, %{details: %{reason: :busy}}} =
             Jido.Exec.run(TestFlowsTour.Gateway, params, %{calls: calls}, max_attempts: 3)

    assert Agent.get(calls, & &1) == 1
  end
end

%{total: 2, failures: 0} = run_tests.()
```

## Assert telemetry

Attach with a unique handler ID, send events to the test process, and detach in `on_exit`. Use `async: false`, because a telemetry handler sees events from every process.

```elixir
defmodule TestFlowsTour.TelemetryTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  def forward(event, _measurements, metadata, {owner, tag}), do: send(owner, {tag, event, metadata})

  test "each component reports one Action span" do
    tag = make_ref()
    handler = {__MODULE__, tag}

    :ok = :telemetry.attach_many(handler, [[:jido, :action, :stop], [:jido, :flow, :stop]], &__MODULE__.forward/4, {self(), tag})
    on_exit(fn -> :telemetry.detach(handler) end)

    assert {:ok, _output, _effects} =
             Jido.Exec.run(TestFlowsTour.ApproveExpense, %{expense_id: "e-6", cents: 10})

    assert_receive {^tag, [:jido, :action, :stop], %{node_name: "score", outcome: :ok}}
    assert_receive {^tag, [:jido, :action, :stop], %{node_name: "record", outcome: :ok}}
    assert_receive {^tag, [:jido, :flow, :stop], %{flow: "test_flows_tour_approve_expense", effect_count: 1}}
  end
end

%{total: 1, failures: 0} = run_tests.()
```

## Test direct Runic steps

Start a Runner with `start_supervised!/1`, so it stops with the test. Use `dispatch_mode: :manual` to control each unit, and a hook to know when a unit has finished. Then assert that a stepped run and an immediate run agree.

Build a unique run ID for each test, since a live ID cannot start twice.

```elixir
defmodule TestFlowsTour.ManagedTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  setup do
    runner = Module.concat(__MODULE__, "Runner#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")
    start_supervised!({Runic.Runner, name: runner})
    {:ok, runner: runner}
  end

  test "manual steps reach the same result as an immediate run", %{runner: runner} do
    input = %{expense_id: "e-7", cents: 10}
    owner = self()
    hooks = [on_complete: fn _runnable, _duration, _state -> send(owner, :unit_done) end]
    id = "expense-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

    assert {:ok, instruction} = Jido.Instruction.resolve(TestFlowsTour.ApproveExpense, input)
    assert {:ok, workflow} = Jido.Exec.compile(instruction, [])

    assert {:ok, _worker} =
             Runic.Runner.start_workflow(runner, id, workflow,
               dispatch_mode: :manual,
               max_concurrency: 1,
               hooks: hooks
             )

    execution_id = Runic.Identity.digest(:execution, id)

    assert {:ok, ^execution_id} =
             Runic.Runner.start_execution(
               runner,
               id,
               Jido.Exec.Fact.local_value(instruction.params),
               execution_id: execution_id,
               run_context: %{_global: instruction.context}
             )

    workflow = step_until_complete(runner, id)
    assert {:ok, observation} = Runic.Runner.execution(runner, id, execution_id)

    assert Jido.Exec.result(workflow, observation) ==
             Jido.Exec.run(TestFlowsTour.ApproveExpense, input)
  end

  defp step_until_complete(runner, id) do
    case Runic.Runner.step(runner, id) do
      :ok ->
        assert_receive :unit_done, 1_000
        step_until_complete(runner, id)

      {:error, :not_runnable} ->
        {:ok, workflow} = Runic.Runner.get_workflow(runner, id)
        workflow
    end
  end
end

%{total: 1, failures: 0} = run_tests.()
```

## Avoid common test problems

- Do not sleep and hope. Wait on a message from an Action or hook with `assert_receive`.
- A blocking Action under `Jido.Exec.run/4` blocks your test, because the call has no caller-side timeout. Pass `:timeout`, or send the Action a release message.
- Durable runs reject PIDs in context. Pass a registered name, then look it up in the Action with `Process.whereis/1`.
- To test waits, start a durable Agent as shown in Flow Waits. Set `after_ms: 0` on a `timer` to test expiry without a delay.

